#: R/interrater.b.R
msgid ""
"An absolute-agreement ICC of {icc_a} means raters' raw scores can be used "
"interchangeably (e.g. averaged into a single composite score) with "
"reasonable confidence; the higher consistency-type ICC ({icc_c}) means their "
"RANKING of cases is more trustworthy than their absolute scale."
msgstr ""
"Un ICC de acuerdo absoluto de {icc_a} significa que los puntajes brutos de "
"los jueces sí pueden usarse de forma intercambiable (p. ej. promediados en "
"un puntaje compuesto único) con confianza razonable; el ICC tipo "
"consistencia más alto ({icc_c}) indica que su ORDENAMIENTO de los casos es "
"más confiable que su escala absoluta."

#: R/interrater.b.R
msgid ""
"An absolute-agreement ICC of {icc_a} means raters' raw scores cannot yet be "
"used interchangeably (e.g. averaged into a single composite score) with "
"reasonable confidence; the higher consistency-type ICC ({icc_c}) means their "
"RANKING of cases is more trustworthy than their absolute scale."
msgstr ""
"Un ICC de acuerdo absoluto de {icc_a} significa que los puntajes brutos de "
"los jueces todavía no pueden usarse de forma intercambiable (p. ej. "
"promediados en un puntaje compuesto único) con confianza razonable; el ICC "
"tipo consistencia más alto ({icc_c}) indica que su ORDENAMIENTO de los casos "
"es más confiable que su escala absoluta."

#: R/fiabilitylibrary.b.R
msgid ""
"An alternative construct-reliability coefficient, H = "
"&Sigma;(λ²/(1&minus;λ²)) / [1 + &Sigma;(λ²/(1&minus;λ²))], computed from the "
"same standardized loadings as CR/ω (Hancock &amp; Mueller, 2001). H is "
"monotonically related to each item's own loading in a way CR/ω is not, "
"making it somewhat less sensitive to adding or removing a single weak "
"indicator — reported alongside CR/ω, not as a replacement for it."
msgstr ""
"Un coeficiente de confiabilidad de constructo alternativo, H = "
"&Sigma;(λ²/(1&minus;λ²)) / [1 + &Sigma;(λ²/(1&minus;λ²))], calculado a "
"partir de las mismas cargas estandarizadas que el CR/ω (Hancock &amp; "
"Mueller, 2001). El H se relaciona monótonamente con la carga de cada ítem de "
"una forma en que el CR/ω no lo hace, haciéndolo algo menos sensible a "
"agregar o quitar un único indicador débil — se reporta junto al CR/ω, no "
"como su reemplazo."

